# Laravel change input value

Attempting to modify a value retrieved from the request object, such as `Input::get('inputname')`, will invariably fail because you are trying to assign a new value to a function's return result. In PHP, functions like `Input::get()` are designed purely for retrieval; they do not maintain internal state or reference variables that can be altered outside their scope. When you execute code like `Input::get('inputname') = "new value";`, the parser throws a `Can't use function return value in write context` error because the expression returns a value, not a variable.

The core misunderstanding here lies in how Laravel handles input data. The `Input` facade (or the newer `$request` instance introduced in later versions) acts as a read-only snapshot of the incoming HTTP request at that specific moment in time. It is not a mutable object like an array or a model you can edit directly. Even if you could technically change the underlying variable holding the input, doing so within a controller method would only affect the current execution flow and would not persist globally.

If your goal is to retrieve a value and then use a modified version of that data for subsequent logic in the same request cycle, you should simply assign the retrieved value to a local variable before modifying it. This is the standard pattern for manipulating incoming data:

```php
$inputName = Input::get('inputname'); // Retrieve the original value
$newValue = "new value";              // Define the new value

// Use $newValue in your logic
// ...

// If you need to pass the modified value back into a view or another method,
// use it directly. There is no need to update Input::get().
```

This approach keeps your code predictable and avoids side effects. Modifying a local copy ensures that the original request data remains intact should you need to reference it later for debugging or validation purposes.

However, if your intent is to permanently alter the input value so that `Input::get('inputname')` returns the new data in subsequent calls within the same script, you are facing a fundamental architectural limitation of web frameworks. HTTP requests are immutable; once the server receives them, the data cannot be changed back into the request object. The framework relies on this immutability to ensure thread safety and consistent behavior across middleware.

If you find yourself needing to persist modified input values across multiple controllers or views within a single application lifecycle, you might consider storing the processed value in a session variable or a server-side cache (like Redis). This allows you to retrieve the amended data using `Session::get('inputname')` or a cached key instead of relying on the original request input.

```php
// Store modified value in session for later retrieval
$request->session()->put('modified_input', "new value");

// Later in another controller or view
$amendedValue = $request->session()->get('modified_input');
```

It is also worth noting that if you are using the newer Laravel syntax where `Input` has been replaced by `$request`, the principle remains identical. You cannot do `$request->input('name') = 'new'`. Instead, you work with the `$request` instance's methods like `merge()` or `except()` if you need to manipulate the underlying input array before validation, though these are typically used during form processing rather than arbitrary runtime changes.

For developers looking for deeper insights into handling request data and maintaining application state, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer guidance on best practices for managing input in modern Laravel applications.
